    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Randsburg grew from a 1895 gold camp called Rand Camp, renamed in 1896 after a South African mining region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        4,
        5,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Randsburg%2C_California",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "By 1896, Randsburg’s population had swelled to over 1,500, turning the desert camp into a bustling boomtown.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Randsburg%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Yellow Aster Mine, found in 1895, ignited Randsburg’s gold rush and still stands as a historic landmark.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://digital-desert.com/randsburg-ca/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Each September, Randsburg celebrates Western Days with gun fights, gold panning, live bands, and dancing.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Randsburg%2C_California",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town’s historic General Store, complete with a soda fountain, and The Vault burger spot preserve 19th‑century charm.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://secretlosangeles.com/randsburg-california-living-ghost-town/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Randsburg’s old railroad and surviving original buildings give it a ghost‑town vibe that has drawn film crews, including for the 1913 movie “The Mystery of Yellow Aster Mine.”.",
